<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tipu Sultan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1751">1751</a>–<a href="/years/1799">1799</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Tiger of Mysore</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tipu <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> was ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a> from 1782, succeeding his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyder-ali">Hyder Ali</a>. Allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, he fought the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> in three wars, attacked Travancore in 1789 and lost half his kingdom in 1792. He was killed defending his capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srirangapatna">Seringapatam</a> in 1799, after which the British restored the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wadiyar-dynasty">Wodeyar dynasty</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tipu-sultan.jpg" alt="Tipu Sultan" width="320" height="454" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anonymous artist from Mysore, India. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TipuSultan1790.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Hyderabad fought alongside the British against <b>Tipu Sultan</b> of Mysore in 1790–1792 and again in 1799.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hyderabad-state">Hyderabad State · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Hyder Ali and <b>Tipu Sultan</b> the state took a strong hand in the economy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-mysore-1399-1799">Kingdom of Mysore (1399-1799) · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Dharma Raja, who refused to surrender Hindu refugees from Mysore's occupation of Malabar, <b>Tipu Sultan</b>'s army entered Cochin from Coimbatore in November 1789 and on 28 December attacked the Nedunkotta and was defeated at the Battle of Nedumkotta.</blockquote><a href="/empire/travancore">Travancore · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>British and allied troops breached the walls of Mysore's capital, and <b>Tipu Sultan</b> died fighting at a gate.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-4">4 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Coorg resisted Hyder Ali and <b>Tipu Sultan of Mysore</b>, who occupied it at intervals from the 1770s.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-coorg">Kingdom of Coorg</a></li><li><blockquote>After <b>Tipu Sultan</b> was killed at Seringapatam in 1799, the British restored the Hindu Wodeyar dynasty, which Hyder Ali had sidelined, and placed the five-year-old Krishnaraja Wodeyar III on the throne under the diwan Purnaiah.</blockquote><a href="/state/mysore-state">Mysore State</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Devanahalli, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srirangapatna">Srirangapatna</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=13.23,77.7&amp;year=1782" aria-label="Devanahalli on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6681 1671 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Tipu Sultan"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7131 1941h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7131 1941h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=13.23,77.7&amp;year=1782">What was here in 1782? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
